From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: natemu <natemu@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] FEXCore: slice1 address guard, split-store check and
 branch-target check

Every change is guarded by fexc_slice1; the code is in
tools/fexc/shim/fexc_sites.inc and fexc_precision.h.

JIT: Arm64JITCore::FexcBeforeOp emits, before each guest memory op, the
flag-free address guard (eor x0, A, A, lsl #16; tbnz x0, #63) and, for a
store wider than one byte, the 4 KiB split check (add x0, A, #w-1;
eor x0, x0, A; tbnz x0, #12). FexcAfterOp also checks the target of an
indirect exit after its definition (or at the instruction start for a
static-register target). The branches go to out-of-line brk stubs that
FexcEmitStubs places after the block body; each stub is a guard or split
site. A far-branch restart discards the attempt's GuestOpcodes and
Subblocks.

Push stores from its unchanged base (stur, then sub for the new stack
pointer) whenever the new pointer is another register or the value is
the base, so nothing is written between the checks and the store.

Register allocation: FexcPrecision::Prescan records, while operands are
still SSA nodes, the RIP-relative accesses (checked at compile time), the
read-modify-write stores that reuse their load's address and the
indirect exit targets.

Dispatcher: ret loads its return address without writeback, so the
target check runs before RSP is committed.
